## Examples

The following examples are provided for clarity. If there is a conflict between these examples and the license terms above, the license terms control.

### Allowed without a separate commercial license

- A company self-hosts SEA-Forge to govern its own internal AI agents.
- A company uses SEA-Forge internally to generate policies, documentation, code, schemas, manifests, tests, workflows, architecture artifacts, cognitive artifacts, knowledge graphs, or operational artifacts for its own operations.
- A company modifies SEA-Forge for its own internal use and does not provide SEA-Forge or SEA-Forge functionality to third parties.
- An individual uses SEA-Forge for personal research, experimentation, or learning.
- A university or nonprofit uses SEA-Forge for non-commercial research or education.
- A consultant uses SEA-Forge internally to manage the consultant’s own internal workflows, repositories, agents, governance processes, and operations.
- A company uses SEA-Forge internally to generate proprietary software or internal tools for its own organization.

### Requires a separate commercial license

- A company hosts SEA-Forge and charges customers to access it.
- A company embeds SEA-Forge into a paid SaaS product.
- A company white-labels SEA-Forge as its own governance, compliance, automation, software-generation, organizational-twin, or agent-management platform.
- A consulting firm uses SEA-Forge to govern, monitor, automate, control, or manage client systems or client AI agents as part of a paid service.
- A managed service provider runs SEA-Forge on behalf of one or more customers.
- A software vendor distributes SEA-Forge or modified SEA-Forge as part of a commercial product.
- A company offers SEA-Forge-powered compliance automation, AI-agent governance, software-generation automation, organizational-twin services, runtime governance, or managed governance services to third parties.
- A company provides customers direct or indirect access to SEA-Forge through a hosted control plane, API, user interface, agent runtime, governance runtime, automation service, or managed deployment.

